 "label": "Meals at home",
 "topic": "Food & drink",
 "rationale": "A meal at home from start to finish: cooking it, calling out that it's ready, eating it, and letting it go cold.",
 "mixUps": "gohan means the meal here, not only rice: gohan tsukuru is cooking dinner and gohan dekita yo means it's ready. Hot food cooling off is sameru (samechau yo), not samuku naru, which is for weather and people.",
 "targets": [
  {
   "id": "c-gohan-o-taberu",
   "romaji": "gohan o taberu",
   "polite": "gohan o tabemasu",
   "english": "eat, have a meal",
   "ja": "ご飯を食べる",
   "kana": "ごはんをたべる",
   "politeJa": "ご飯を食べます",
   "noun": "gohan",
   "particle": "o",
   "verb": "taberu",
   "verbJa": "食べる",
   "verbSense": "eat",
   "englishVerb": "eat",
   "trap": "Learners who know gohan as 'rice' avoid it for a pasta or burger dinner, but it is the everyday word for any meal (it means rice only when set against pan or men); shokuji (o) suru is stiffer.",
   "partners": [
    "asagohan (breakfast)",
    "ohiru (lunch)",
    "yuuhan (dinner)",
    "bangohan (dinner)"
   ],
   "flags": [],
   "use": "Said about any meal, not just rice. gohan tabeta? (have you eaten?) is a real question between family, partners and close friends, not a greeting as in Korean or Chinese; answer un, tabeta, or mada (tabetenai) for 'not yet', because tabenakatta means you skipped the meal. Casual speech drops o; the meal is named only when it matters (asagohan, ohiru, bangohan).",
   "notes": "meshi kuu (meshi kutta?) is the rough, mostly male version. Skipping a meal, by choice or habit, is nuku: asagohan nuita (I skipped breakfast).",
   "example": {
    "romaji": "gohan tabete kara iku ne.",
    "ja": "ご飯食べてから行くね。",
    "english": "I'll head over after I eat."
   },
   "examplePolite": {
    "romaji": "gohan o tabete kara ikimasu ne.",
    "ja": "ご飯を食べてから行きますね。",
    "english": "I'll come over after I've eaten."
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "c-gohan-o-tsukuru",
   "romaji": "gohan o tsukuru",
   "polite": "gohan o tsukurimasu",
   "english": "cook a meal, make dinner",
   "ja": "ご飯を作る",
