    "ryouri (a dish)",
    "karee (curry)"
   ],
   "flags": [],
   "use": "The everyday way to say who's cooking: kyou wa watashi ga gohan tsukuru, or gohan tsukutte kureta (they made me dinner). Casual speech drops o, and the meal is named only when it matters (bangohan, obentou). ryouri suru also works for tonight's cooking (kyou wa boku ga ryouri suru), but it leans toward cooking as an activity (ryouri suru no ga suki).",
   "notes": "Cooking the rice itself is gohan o taku (in a rice cooker).",
   "example": {
    "romaji": "kyou wa watashi ga gohan tsukuru ne.",
    "ja": "今日は私がご飯作るね。",
    "english": "I'll make dinner tonight."
   },
   "examplePolite": {
    "romaji": "kyou wa watashi ga gohan o tsukurimasu ne.",
    "ja": "今日は私がご飯を作りますね。",
    "english": "I'll make dinner tonight."
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "c-gohan-ga-dekiru",
   "romaji": "gohan ga dekiru",
   "polite": "gohan ga dekimasu",
   "english": "the meal gets ready (dekita = it's ready)",
   "ja": "ご飯ができる",
   "kana": "ごはんができる",
   "politeJa": "ご飯ができます",
   "noun": "gohan",
   "particle": "ga",
   "verb": "dekiru",
   "verbJa": "できる",
   "verbSense": "come into being, get done; also 'can do' and 'be ready'",
   "englishVerb": "be ready",
   "trap": "English 'dinner's ready' is in the present, but the kitchen call says the meal has come into being: gohan dekita yo; plain gohan dekiru yo means it'll be ready soon. The other everyday call is gohan da yo! (food's on!). If you reach for junbi, it's gohan no junbi (ga) dekita, the same dekiru as junbi dekita? (are you ready?).",
   "partners": [
    "ryouri (a dish: ryouri dekita; ryouri dekiru? asks if you can cook)",
    "obentou (packed lunch)",
    "junbi (preparations: junbi dekita?)"
   ],
   "flags": [],
   "use": "Called out from the kitchen: gohan dekita yo! (dinner's ready!), in the past tense and usually without ga. mou sugu dekiru yo (it's nearly ready) uses the non-past dekiru for what's coming.",
   "notes": "gohan dekiteru kara (the food's made) means it's already waiting for you. gohan yo! is the motherly, feminine version of gohan da yo. The rice cooker finishing is gohan ga taketa.",
   "example": {
    "romaji": "gohan dekita yo! hayaku kite.",
    "ja": "ご飯できたよ！早く来て。",
    "english": "Dinner's ready! Come quick."
   },
   "examplePolite": {
    "romaji": "gohan ga dekimashita yo. douzo, samenai uchi ni.",
    "ja": "ご飯ができましたよ。どうぞ、冷めないうちに。",
    "english": "Dinner's ready. Please, have it while it's hot."
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "c-gohan-ga-sameru",
   "romaji": "gohan ga sameru",
   "polite": "gohan ga samemasu",
   "english": "the food gets cold",
   "ja": "ご飯が冷める",
   "kana": "ごはんがさめる",
   "politeJa": "ご飯が冷めます",
   "noun": "gohan",
   "particle": "ga",
   "verb": "sameru",
   "verbJa": "冷める",
   "verbSense": "lose heat and cool down (food, drinks, bath water)",
   "englishVerb": "get cold",
   "trap": "Not samuku naru: samui is for weather and how you feel, and hot food or drink cooling off is sameru. tsumetaku naru is for something that has gone fully cold.",
   "partners": [
    "koohii (coffee)",
